{"accepted": null, "attempt": 0, "campaign_id": "pat-19-x3screen-1", "campaign_sha256": "95b7a0717ecfcc28b6595e4a88dee108d158ff7d85dddc84f7d1c56de8d5fd1d", "cloud_executions": 0, "cloud_sessions": [], "dry_run": false, "envelope_sha256": "d3eaf1de26eb36e5e0b6740391923a67f4b87f2025895fe19329c2f8aa86c8a8", "exploration": {"bundle_modified": false, "refusal": null, "report": {"files": ["plugins/foundry/tooling/foundry/frame.py", "plugins/foundry/tests/test_linear_tracker.py"], "functions": [{"file": "plugins/foundry/tooling/foundry/frame.py", "name": "frame.materialize"}], "rationale": "frame.materialize() (plugins/foundry/tooling/foundry/frame.py, lines 34-93) performs a preflight that resolves existing ADRs via tr.list_adrs(p) and validates that every constrained_by reference exists (by title or index). The bug is that it does NOT check the ADR status field. Per the Adr dataclass in models.py (line 47), status can be 'proposed', 'accepted', 'deprecated', or 'superseded'. When a referenced existing ADR has status 'deprecated' or 'superseded', the frame proceeds to create issues linked to dead ADRs, producing partial links. The fix: within the existing preflight block (lines 42-54), after building existing_adrs, iterate over each issue's constrained_by references, look up the referenced ADR in existing_adrs (by matching title), and if its status is 'deprecated' or 'superseded', raise ValueError before any write. This blocks partial issue creation. The test file (plugins/foundry/tests/test_linear_tracker.py) needs a regression test mirroring test_linear_frame_rejects_any_unknown_adr_before_first_write (line 2360) but seeding an existing ADR with status='deprecated' or 'superseded' and verifying frame.materialize raises ValueError with wire.issues/wire.documents/wire.comments unchanged."}, "report_source": "final_message", "score": {"counts": {"correct_files": 2, "counted_functions": 1, "found_files": 1, "found_functions": 0, "reported_files": 2, "truth_files": 1, "truth